== The Warfaa Dynasty ==

=== Rise ===
'''[[Gobaad]]''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "freedom") reunified the Ufyen heartland around 150 BCE, following the collapse of the [[Dalmar Period|Barre Dynasty]] and a period of highland fragmentation. The kingdom was smaller than under any previous dynasty: the Warfaa kings controlled [[Lake Haro]], the [[Kushish]] iron mines, and the Upper [[Buran River]] valley, but exercised little influence beyond the highlands. The coastal cities were by this time fully independent, and the [[Kingdom of Sago]] dominated the western lowlands.

=== Defensive consolidation ===
The Warfaa kings strengthened the fortifications of [[Badalga-Jir]] and [[Jowhar]], maintained the ''[[Kaas|kaas]]'' garrison network, and invested in the Lake Haro irrigation system. The court cultivated a revivalist ideology, promoting the memory of the [[Geedi Period|Geedi]] golden age and emphasising the king's role as chief priest of [[Waaq]]. The summit shrine of [[Buur Waaq]] was renovated and expanded, and seasonal sacrificial rituals were elaborated.

=== End of the Warfaa Dynasty ===
'''[[Ina-Waaq]]''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "son of Waaq") was the last Warfaa king. The dynasty was succeeded by the Cigaal around 100 AD.

== The Cigaal Dynasty ==

The '''Cigaal Dynasty''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: ''Cigaal'', "defiance") governed from approximately 100 to 250 AD. The Cigaal maintained the defensive posture established by the Warfaa, holding the highland interior against the growing power of the coastal city-states. The period saw the continued expansion of Mesogean trade on the southern coast, which increasingly marginalised the highland economy.

== The Liibaan Dynasty ==

The '''Liibaan Dynasty''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: ''Liibaan'', "victory") governed from approximately 250 to 400 AD. The name is considered ironic by modern historians: the Liibaan presided over the most consequential shift in the peninsula's political landscape. The formation of the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] in 403 AD — uniting the [[Republic of Mazar]] and the [[Kingdom of Iskhal]] under [[Bugad the Great]] — created a coastal power larger and wealthier than any previous polity. The Liibaan court was unable to respond to this shift, and internal factions within the highland aristocracy challenged the dynasty's leadership. Within a generation, the Liibaan were replaced by the [[Saleebaan Period|Saleebaan]].

